# Target C-II — propagate the corrected `g=0.0076` gap through restored rhombus energy

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative propagation; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** fully force-visible no-container/full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited corrected `g=0.0076` matched atomic theorem; hostile-audited complement-rhombus incidence and restored dominant-energy stack  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected uniform strong-branch theorem raises the matched atomic input to

\[
\boxed{
C_{76}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.}
\tag{G76.1}

The complement-rhombus incidence and restored-energy theorems are symbolic in this input.  Substituting `C_76` raises the safe full-core Type-II floor from the hostile-audited `g=0.0071` value `1.84323` to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.84332.}
\tag{G76.2}

The unique scalar crossing is

\[
1.84332993800832\ldots .
\tag{G76.3}

The round value `1.84332` is claimed; `1.84333` lies just above the crossing and is not claimed.

---

## 1. Symbolic restored-energy stack

Write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

and

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2.
\tag{G76.4}

The network envelope and (G76.1) give

\[
C_{76}\le U+o(1),
\]

hence

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}
\le {9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{76}+o(1).
\tag{G76.5}

Retain the two-bin functions

\[
m_2(U)={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2},
\qquad
r(U)=U-m_2(U).
\tag{G76.6}

The already-audited shared-retention theorem supplies retained dominant outer-occurrence mass `z` and retained complement O--O pair mass `p` with

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge
S(t,\delta,\kappa):=
\pi-{t+\kappa\over2}-r(U)-o(1).}
\tag{G76.7}

The dominant selected occurrences pay

\[
F(z)D-o(D),
\qquad
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
\tag{G76.8}

of actual outer directional energy.  If `e_cD` is the exact terminal energy on the disjoint retained complement pairs, then

\[
e_c\le\delta-F(z)+o(1).
\tag{G76.9}

The complement-rhombus incidence theorem gives

\[
p(1-\delta)-{e_c\over2}
\le {r(U)^2\over2}+o(1).
\tag{G76.10}

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
p\le
{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1).}
\tag{G76.11}

Combining (G76.7) and (G76.11), every survivor satisfies

\[
G(z;t,\delta,\kappa):=
 z+{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}
\ge S(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{G76.12}

---

## 2. Minimal dominant retained mass

Throughout the certified range below, `delta<0.05` and `0<=z<=pi`.  Hence

\[
G_z
=1-{F'(z)\over2(1-\delta)}
>0.929.
\tag{G76.13}

Thus `G` is strictly increasing in `z`.  Let `Z_*(t,delta,kappa)` be the unique threshold satisfying

\[
G(Z_*;t,\delta,\kappa)=S(t,\delta,\kappa).
\tag{G76.14}

Every survivor must have `z>=Z_*-o(1)`.  Since the dominant selected energy is part of the total outer detour,

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge F(Z_*)-o(1).}
\tag{G76.15}

---

## 3. Feasible-region reduction

The companion certificate covers

\[
1.84323\le t\le1.84334
\]

and the whole feasible region (G76.5).  It gives

\[
C_{76}\le U<2.012,
\qquad
0\le\delta<0.050,
\tag{G76.16}

\[
0<r(U)<0.111,
\qquad
-0.062<m_2'(U)<0.
\tag{G76.17}

Put `r'(U)=1-m_2'(U)`, so

\[
1<r'(U)<1.062.
\tag{G76.18}

### `kappa` direction

At fixed `t,delta`, `U_kappa=-1/2`.  Since

\[
S_\kappa=-{m_2'(U)\over2}>0,
\qquad
G_U={r(U)r'(U)\over1-\delta}>0,
\]

implicit differentiation of (G76.14) gives

\[
G_z Z_{*,\kappa}=S_\kappa+{G_U\over2}>0.
\]

Hence `Z_*` and the contradiction margin increase with `kappa`.  The weakest point has

\[
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
\tag{G76.19}

### `delta` direction

At fixed `t,kappa`, `U_delta=-2` and

\[
S_\delta=2r'<2.124.
\]

The positive direct partial derivative of `G` with respect to `delta` may be discarded when upper-bounding `Z_{*,delta}`.  The certificate gives

\[
G_U<0.125,
\qquad
G_z>0.929,
\]

and therefore

\[
Z_{*,\delta}<2.56.
\tag{G76.20}

Since

\[
F'(Z_*)\le1-{\sqrt3\over2}<0.134,
\]

\[
{d\over d\delta}[F(Z_*)-\delta]
<2.56(0.134)-1<-0.65.
\tag{G76.21}

Thus maximal detour is weakest.  Together with (G76.19) and (G76.5),

\[
\boxed{
\kappa=0,
\qquad
\delta={1\over2}\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{76}\right),
\qquad
U=C_{76}.}
\tag{G76.22}

---

## 4. Boundary scalar

Put

\[
M_0=m_2(C_{76}),
\qquad
r_0=C_{76}-M_0.
\]

The certificate gives, for orientation,

\[
C_{76}=1.9122256137279146\ldots,
\]

\[
M_0=1.9046103897733408\ldots,
\qquad
r_0=0.0076152239545738\ldots .
\tag{G76.23}

At the weak corner define

\[
\delta_0(t)
={1\over2}\left({9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{76}\right),
\]

\[
S_0(t)=\pi-{t\over2}-r_0.
\tag{G76.24}

Let `Z_0(t)` be the unique solution of

\[
Z_0+{\delta_0-F(Z_0)+r_0^2\over2(1-\delta_0)}
=S_0(t).
\tag{G76.25}

Every survivor requires

\[
H_0(t):=F(Z_0(t))-\delta_0(t)\le0.
\tag{G76.26}

The preceding monotonicity makes `H_0` strictly decreasing.

---

## 5. Certified crossing

The high-precision verifier

`research/verify_target_c_g0076_energy_restored_rhombus_propagation.py`

checks

\[
H_0(1.84332)
>2.35\times10^{-5}>0,
\]

\[
H_0(1.84334)
<-2.38\times10^{-5}<0,
\]

and the tight signs

\[
H_0(1.84332993800832)>0,
\]

\[
H_0(1.84332993800833)<0.
\]

Therefore the unique scalar crossing lies in

\[
\boxed{
1.84332993800832<t_*<1.84332993800833.}
\tag{G76.27}

This proves the safe floor (G76.2).

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The corrected `0.0076` matched gap increases the low-`t` floor by another

\[
1.8433299380\ldots-1.8432353529\ldots
=9.4585\times10^{-5}
\]

beyond the hostile-audited `g=0.0071` propagation.  That scalar gain is modest.  The larger value is structural: the stronger atomic and rhombic floors can now be fed into the dominant double-outer-train bootstrap and the near-saturated three-phase boundary order.

The previous structural theorem forced a linear dominant O--O family only for `t<=1.843`, a window now below the scalar floor.  The next theorem should extend that bootstrap above `1.84332`, so the forced O--O family remains nonvacuous in the surviving interval.

No exact equilateral triangle or strict sub-`D` competitor is claimed.  C-II remains open.
